&nbsp;

This is one thing I&#39;ll admit I&#39;ve never gotten - Rook City Wraith&#39;s power. &nbsp;Unless I have some other hero who can stack the environment deck, it seems largely useless, and potentially dangerous.

&nbsp;

This one-shot is a case in point. &nbsp;In Megalopolis, there are several cards (Hostage Situation and Paparazzi come to mind) that will really screw your day. However, if you use RC Wraith&#39;s power to try to uncover these cards, and you reveal (ex:) some environment card that&#39;s not so bad, you can&#39;t just leave it there; you have to play or discard it. &nbsp;You probably won&#39;t play it (because most environment cards are not good for the heroes, and the less of them in play, the better), and if you discard it, you&#39;ve now gotten rid of a not-so-bad environment card from the top of the deck, <b>increasing</b>&nbsp;the chances that the next card <b>will</b>&nbsp;be one of the ones you wanted to avoid. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

Any math/statistics/probability geniuses out there ever work this out? &nbsp;On the whole, probability-wise, does RC Wraith&#39;s power help you by getting rid of bad cards, or does it just work to discard not-so-bad environment cards, and bring the bad cards in the environment deck that much closer?
